            <ArticleTitle>Emergency department evaluation and management of foot and ankle pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>363-96</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.emc.2014.12.008</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0733-8627(14)00133-3</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Foot and ankle injuries are a frequent cause for a visit to the Emergency Department. A thorough evaluation and treatment of these injuries needs to be an area of in-depth familiarity for the Emergency Medicine physician. The key to proper evaluation is first a history and physical examination that focuses on determining what, if any, imaging is required. Subsequently, a focused history, physical examination, and imaging will then determine if an injury is stable or unstable, requiring operative intervention.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Published by Elsevier Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
